    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe consolidation itself is well-executed and idiomatic: collapsing three `is\u2026InUse` methods into one `isIdentityKeyInUse`, exposing the key as a `@Transient` derived getter on `Owner` (matching the existing `householdMemberCount` pattern), hoisting the `sharesHousehold` block above the duplicate check so `householdId` is populated before the key is computed, and deleting the now-dead `normalizeTelephone`/`normalizeEmail` helpers plus their imports. Logic lives in the right units and nothing is duplicated. One structural nit keeps it from being clean:\n\n**Inconsistent / misleading local naming in `Owner.getIdentityKey()` (Owner.java:163-166).** The three components are built as a parallel block, but the names don't line up:\n\n```java\nString normalizedTelephone = this.telephone == null ? \"\" : this.telephone;\nString emailOrEmpty        = this.email == null ? \"\" : this.email;\nString householdOrEmpty    = this.householdId == null ? \"\" : this.householdId;\n```\n\nThe first variable is named `normalizedTelephone`, implying this method normalizes the telephone, when it does no such thing \u2014 it just substitutes empty-for-null exactly like the other two. The actual E.164 normalization happens upstream in `OwnerMapper` (that invariant is what the Javadoc leans on). A maintainer reading only this method could reasonably infer normalization occurs here and be misled.\n\nSuggested fix: rename to `telephoneOrEmpty` so all three follow the same `*OrEmpty` pattern and none of the names overstate what the method does. The Javadoc already documents that the components arrive pre-normalized, so the local name doesn't need to carry that claim.",
